    {
     "type": "key",
     "style": "methode",
     "title": "Rounding",
     "md": "To round to the nearest 10 (or 100, 1 000) look at the digit just to the right. If it is **5 or more**, round up; if it is **less than 5**, keep the digit and replace the rest with zeros. Estimating means rounding each number first, then calculating quickly."
    },
    {
     "type": "key",
     "style": "pieges",
     "title": "Common mistakes",
     "md": "- Calculating from left to right and ignoring BODMAS.\n- Rounding 4 876 to the nearest hundred as 4 800: the next digit is 7, so the answer is 4 900.\n- Forgetting that 6 ÷ 2 × 3 is worked from left to right: it is 9, not 1."
    },
    {
     "type": "example",
     "title": "Example 1: BODMAS",
     "statement": "Calculate 8 + 4 × (12 − 7)² ÷ 10.",
     "steps": [
      "Brackets first: 12 − 7 = 5.",
      "Order: 5² = 25. The sum is now 8 + 4 × 25 ÷ 10.",
      "Multiply and divide from the left: 4 × 25 = 100, then 100 ÷ 10 = 10.",
      "Add: 8 + 10 = 18."
     ],
     "answer": "**18**"
    },
    {
     "type": "example",
     "title": "Example 2: rounding and estimating",
     "statement": "Round 4 876 to the nearest 10 and the nearest 100. Then estimate 28 × 52 and compare with the exact answer.",
     "steps": [
      "Nearest 10: the units digit is 6 (5 or more), so 4 876 → 4 880.",
      "Nearest 100: the tens digit is 7, so 4 876 → 4 900.",
      "Estimate: 28 ≈ 30 and 52 ≈ 50, so 30 × 50 = 1 500.",
      "Exact: 28 × 52 = 1 456. The estimate is close, so the answer is reasonable."
     ],
     "answer": "**4 880; 4 900; estimate 1 500, exact 1 456.**"
    }
   ],
